  "Question": "What is the remainder when \\(x^{51}+51\\) is divided by \\(x+1?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(0\\)",
    "B": "\\(1\\)",
    "C": "\\(49\\)",
    "D": "\\(50\\)",
    "E": "\\(51\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "Because the divisor \\(x+1\\) has degree \\(1\\), the remainder is a constant \\(r\\). Write the division identity as\n\\[\nx^{51}+51=(x+1)Q(x)+r,\n\\]\nwhere \\(Q(x)\\) is the polynomial quotient. Setting \\(x=-1\\) makes the term containing the quotient vanish, leaving\n\\[\nr=(-1)^{51}+51=-1+51=50.\n\\]\nThe remainder is \\(\\boxed{50}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{D}\\).",
